      "worked": "The original denominators require $\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta\\ne0$ and $\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta\\ne0$, so exclude $45^{\\circ},\\ 135^{\\circ},\\ 225^{\\circ},\\ 315^{\\circ}$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta}-\\frac{1}{\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta}&=\\frac{-2\\sin\\theta}{\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta}\\\\\\frac{1}{\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta}-\\frac{1}{\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta}&=\\frac{-2\\sin\\theta}{1-2\\sin^2\\theta}\\end{align*}$$\n\nEquating this to $1$ and multiplying by the nonzero denominator gives\n\n$$\\begin{align*}-2\\sin\\theta&=1-2\\sin^2\\theta\\\\2\\sin^2\\theta-2\\sin\\theta-1&=0\\\\\\sin\\theta&=\\frac{2\\pm\\sqrt{4+8}}{4}\\\\\\sin\\theta&=\\frac{1\\pm\\sqrt3}{2}\\end{align*}$$\n\nReject $\\frac{1+\\sqrt3}{2}>1$. Thus $\\sin\\theta=\\frac{1-\\sqrt3}{2}<0$, so the solutions are in quadrants III and IV.\n\nLet $\\alpha=\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3-1}{2}\\right)$, measured in degrees. Keep this value unrounded until the final answers.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\theta&=180^{\\circ}+\\alpha\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\\theta=360^{\\circ}-\\alpha\\\\\\theta&\\approx201.470701^{\\circ}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\\theta\\approx338.529299^{\\circ}\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor these roots, $\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta=-2\\sin\\theta=\\sqrt3-1\\ne0$, so both original denominators are nonzero and both solutions are valid.\n\nHence $\\theta\\approx201^{\\circ},\\ 339^{\\circ}$ to the nearest degree.",
